+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+See `proposal.md` - Why. The service is a small TypeScript app on Node's built-in
+`node:sqlite` (`DatabaseSync`), with a strict layering convention documented in
+`README.md`: `routes/` only parse params and call a service, business rules live
+in `services/`, and all SQL lives in `repositories/`. Time is read from an
+injected `Clock` (`src/clock.ts`), never `new Date()` in services. Errors are
+thrown as `AppError`, which the router renders as
+`{ error: { code, message } }` with the matching HTTP status.
+
+The order and coupon data model already exists in `src/db.ts`; no schema change
+is allowed. `OrderService` already receives the injected `Clock` and is wired in
+`src/app.ts`, so it is the natural home for the coupon rules. Requirements are
+specified in `specs/order-coupon/spec.md` - this document only covers how.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+
+- Implement `POST /orders/:id/apply-coupon` using the existing layering and
+  error conventions, reading the current time from the injected `Clock`.
+- Keep the three success writes (order update, redemption insert, usage-count
+  increment) atomic in a single SQL transaction.
+- Preserve the existing behavior and tests of the current endpoints.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+
+- No schema/table changes and no new dependencies.
+- No changes to coupon administration (`POST /coupons`, `GET /coupons/:code`).
+- No coupon removal/refund, and no re-applying once an order has a coupon.
+- No changes to the existing `pay` / `cancel` flows beyond what already exists.
+
+## Decisions
+
+### Rules live in `OrderService`; add `CouponRepository` to its constructor
+Rationale: the rules need both the order and the coupon, plus the `Clock` that
+is already injected into `OrderService`. Adding `CouponRepository` as a second
+constructor argument keeps the HTTP layer thin and matches the existing wiring
+in `src/app.ts`. Alternative considered: a standalone
+`CouponApplicationService`; rejected because it would duplicate the order lookup
+and status logic already in `OrderService` and add a second wiring point for no
+behavioral gain.
+
+### Normalize the code in the service, check rules in the exact table order
+`applyCoupon` trims and uppercases `couponCode` first, then evaluates checks in
+this fixed sequence so the first failure wins: input validity (400
+`INVALID_REQUEST`) → order exists (404 `ORDER_NOT_FOUND`) → status `PENDING`
+(409 `ORDER_NOT_PENDING`) → order already has a coupon (409
+`COUPON_ALREADY_APPLIED`) → coupon exists (404 `COUPON_NOT_FOUND`) → `ACTIVE`
+(400 `COUPON_INACTIVE`) → `validFrom <= now` (400 `COUPON_NOT_YET_VALID`) →
+`now < validUntil` (400 `COUPON_EXPIRED`) → usage limit (400
+`COUPON_USAGE_EXHAUSTED`) → minimum amount (400 `MINIMUM_NOT_MET`). This mirrors
+the task's error table directly and keeps the ordering explicit and testable.
+
+### Compute the discount in one small pure step
+Base discount is `coupon.value` for FIXED and
+`Math.floor(order.amountCents * coupon.value / 100)` for PERCENT; then apply
+`maxDiscountCents` cap when non-null; then clamp with
+`Math.min(discount, order.amountCents)`. `finalAmountCents` is
+`amountCents - discount`, which is therefore never negative. Integer-only
+arithmetic keeps all money values in cents.
+
+### Persist atomically in a repository method
+Add `OrderRepository.applyCoupon(orderId, couponCode, discountCents,
+finalAmountCents, now)` that wraps `BEGIN` / `COMMIT` around: the `UPDATE orders`
+setting `discount_cents`, `final_amount_cents`, `coupon_code`; the `INSERT INTO
+coupon_redemptions`; and `UPDATE coupons SET used_count = used_count + 1`. On any
+error it issues `ROLLBACK` and rethrows. Rationale: this keeps all SQL in the
+repository layer (per the layering rule) and guarantees the writes succeed or
+fail together. `node:sqlite` is synchronous, so a simple try/catch around the
+statements is sufficient; there is no async interleaving to guard against within
+one request. Alternative considered: exposing a generic transaction helper from
+`db.ts` and calling several repo methods from the service; rejected as more
+machinery than this single operation needs.
+
+### Reuse the existing serialization for the response
+After a successful write the service re-reads the order via `this.get(id)` and
+returns it, so the `200` body is produced by the same `toOrder` mapper as
+`GET /orders/:id` and cannot drift from it. `created_at` on the redemption row
+uses the same `now` value the rules were evaluated with.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- [A failure between the write statements could partially apply the change] →
+  Wrap all three statements in one transaction with `ROLLBACK` in the `catch`,
+  satisfying the "rejection/failure leaves no trace" requirement.
+- [Divergent time between validity check and `created_at`] → Read `this.clock()`
+  once per request and reuse that single `now` for both.
+- [Rules could drift from the documented error order] → Encode the order in one
+  linear method and cover the precedence cases with tests.
